---
import BlogPostLayout from '../../../../layouts/BlogPostLayout.astro';

const lang = 'en-us';
const post = {
  title: 'Amalfi Coast Weather in July and August 2026',
  subtitle: 'Positano and Amalfi in summer: temperatures, terrain shade, packing kit and three weather hacks to stay sane',
  category: 'GUIDES',
  date: 'July 29, 2026',
  readTime: '8 min',
  image: '/images/meteo-costiera-luglio-agosto-hero.webp',
  imageAlt: 'Panoramic view of Positano on the Amalfi Coast in summer',
  path: '/en-us/blog/weather-july-august-2026/',
};

const metaTitle = 'Amalfi Coast Weather July & August 2026 | Positano and Amalfi';
const metaDescription = 'July averages 88°F (31°C), August 90°F (32°C) with 66% humidity. How the microclimate works, which beaches lose sun at 3:30 PM, what to pack and 3 weather hacks for Positano and Amalfi.';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: '/en-us/' },
  { name: 'Blog', url: '/en-us/blog/' },
  { name: 'Weather July and August 2026', url: '/en-us/blog/weather-july-august-2026/' },
];

const quickInfo = [
  { label: 'July avg. high', value: '88°F / 31°C (peaks 93°F)' },
  { label: 'August avg. high', value: '90°F / 32°C (66% humidity)' },
  { label: 'Night low', value: '72-73°F / 22-23°C' },
  { label: 'Sea temperature', value: '77°F Jul, 81°F Aug' },
  { label: 'Daily sunshine', value: '10-11 hours' },
  { label: 'UV index', value: '9-10 (very high)' },
  { label: 'Rain risk', value: 'Rare heat storms late August' },
  { label: 'Local wind', value: '"Faugn" warm night breeze' },
];

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Is July or August hotter on the Amalfi Coast?',
    a: 'August. July averages 88°F (31°C) with drier air, while August stabilises at 90-91°F (32-33°C) with 66-70% humidity and limestone rock that has been storing heat for weeks. The perceived heat, especially in the narrow alleys of Amalfi and Atrani, is noticeably higher in August.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What time does the beach in Amalfi go into shade?',
    a: "Beaches in Amalfi, Atrani and Minori face southeast: the sun disappears behind the mountain around 3:30-4:00 PM in peak summer. Great if you want an afternoon reprieve, less so if you want to stay in the sun until sunset. In that case head to Positano or Praiano (Gavitella), which stay in sunlight until 7:30-8:00 PM.",
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I really need SPF 50 sunscreen even if I tan easily?',
    a: 'Yes, always. The UV index hits 9-10 in July and August, and the white limestone cliffs reflect light like a mirror. The glare is more aggressive than an average Italian beach: SPF 50, a wide-brim hat and polarised sunglasses are the three allies that will save your first day.',
  },
  {
    q: 'When is the sea warmer, July or August?',
    a: 'August. In July the water hovers at 77°F (25°C), perfect for long swims. In August it peaks at 81°F (27°C), essentially a heated pool, with exceptional underwater visibility on calm days.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does it rain in August on the Amalfi Coast?',
    a: 'Very rarely. August averages 3 days with precipitation, almost always late-afternoon heat storms that last 20-30 minutes, cool the air and leave you with spectacular skies afterward. They rarely ruin a full day, but keep an eye on the radar from 5:00 PM onward.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How should I dress on the Coast in peak summer?',
    a: 'Natural, light fabrics: linen, cotton, silk. Avoid synthetics that increase perspiration. For walking, wear sandals with rubber soles or canvas sneakers with good grip: the limestone steps and Positano pavements get slick from the mix of heat and sea salt. Leave your heels at the hotel.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is it better to travel by bus or ferry between July and August?',
    a: 'Ferry, no question. The SITA bus between 11:00 AM and 3:00 PM in peak summer means waiting under a scorching sun with traffic at a standstill. Travelmar ferries cost more but the sea breeze turns the ride into a ventilated scenic tour. The Salerno-Amalfi-Positano route takes about 60-70 minutes.',
  },
  {
    q: "What is the 'Faugn' of the Amalfi Coast?",
    a: 'It is the local name for a warm, dry breeze that blows down from the mountains at night, typical of summer evenings. Sometimes at 11:00 PM you feel a sudden gust of warm air, especially on moonlight boat tours or on the highest terraces. Always pack a light long-sleeve shirt for evening excursions on the water.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is Ravello really cooler than the coast?',
    a: 'Yes. Sitting at 1,200 feet (365 metres) of elevation, Ravello is consistently 5-7°F (3-4°C) cooler than Amalfi or Positano, with a dry breeze that regenerates. It is the perfect break from the beach heat, especially on the most humid days of late August.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which beach on the Amalfi Coast gets the most sun?',
    a: 'Gavitella in Praiano. Facing west, it receives direct light until 7:30-8:00 PM and watches the sun set straight into the sea behind Capri: it is the only beach on the Coast where you can have an aperitivo in your swimsuit while the sun drops on the marine horizon.',
  },
];

const sidebarLinks = [
  { label: 'Positano', href: '/en-us/amalfi-coast/positano/' },
  { label: 'Amalfi', href: '/en-us/amalfi-coast/amalfi/' },
  { label: 'Praiano', href: '/en-us/amalfi-coast/praiano/' },
  { label: 'Ravello', href: '/en-us/amalfi-coast/ravello/' },
  { label: 'Amalfi Coast Beaches', href: '/en-us/beaches/' },
];

const authorRole = 'Local editorial team · Amalfi Coast';

const relatedArticles = [
  {
    title: 'Sunset Beaches on the Amalfi Coast',
    category: 'DISCOVERIES',
    date: 'May 2, 2026',
    image: '/images/tramonto-costiera.webp',
    imageAlt: 'Sunset beach on the Amalfi Coast',
    path: '/en-us/blog/sunset-beaches/'
  },
  {
    title: 'Comfortable, Well-Equipped Beaches',
    category: 'GUIDES',
    date: 'April 18, 2026',
    image: '/images/spiagge-bambini.webp',
    imageAlt: 'Comfortable equipped beaches on the Amalfi Coast',
    path: '/en-us/blog/comfortable-beaches/'
  }
];

const keyTakeaways = [
  'July averages 88°F (31°C) with dry air and 77°F (25°C) water: the perfect month for tanning. August climbs to 90-91°F (32-33°C) with 66-70% humidity and 81°F (27°C) water.',
  'Beaches in Amalfi, Atrani and Minori lose the sun between 3:30 and 4:00 PM; Positano, Praiano and Gavitella stay lit until evening.',
  'Ravello, at 1,200 feet (365 metres), is always 5-7°F cooler than the coast: the regenerating escape from muggy days.',
  'Late August brings rare 20-30 minute heat storms in the late afternoon: they cool the air and leave spectacular skies behind.',
  'In peak season, Travelmar ferries beat SITA buses on every front: faster, ventilated and with zero risk of waiting in the scorching sun.',
];
---

<BlogPostLayout
  lang={lang}
  post={post}
  metaTitle={metaTitle}
  metaDescription={metaDescription}
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  quickInfo={quickInfo}
  faq={faq}
  sidebarLinks={sidebarLinks}
  authorRole={authorRole}
  relatedArticles={relatedArticles}
  pageKey="weather-july-august-2026"
>

  <!-- Key Takeaways -->
  <div class="bg-slate-900 text-white p-8 rounded-[2rem]">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-lg font-bold text-med-gold uppercase tracking-[0.15em] mb-6">Key Takeaways</h2>
    <ul class="space-y-4">
      {keyTakeaways.map((point) => (
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
          <span class="w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-med-gold flex-shrink-0 mt-2"></span>
          <span class="text-white/80 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">{point}</span>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </div>

  <!-- Intro -->
  <div>
    <p class="text-slate-600 text-xl leading-relaxed mb-6 font-light">
      Right now, summer 2026 is in full swing, and <a href="/en-us/amalfi-coast/positano/" class="text-med-blue hover:underline">Positano</a> and <a href="/en-us/amalfi-coast/amalfi/" class="text-med-blue hover:underline">Amalfi</a> are literally the center of the world. July and August mean crystal-clear water, endless sunsets, and those iconic Dolce Vita vibes. However, they also mean soaring temperatures, a beating sun, and some seriously intense logistics.
    </p>
    <p class="text-slate-600 text-lg leading-relaxed">
      The <a href="/en-us/amalfi-coast/" class="text-med-blue hover:underline">Coast</a> has a unique microclimate: the Lattari Mountains block northern winds, creating a thermal barrier, while the sea turns into a giant heated pool. Here is how to read the weather and plan your days to beat the heat waves and have the trip of a lifetime.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- Il Bollettino Termico -->
  <div>
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6">The Thermal Report: What the Thermometer Really Says</h2>
    <p class="text-slate-600 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Let's be real: it gets hot during these two months. But not all heat is created equal, and the two months have very different personalities.
    </p>

    <div class="bg-white p-8 rounded-[2rem] border border-slate-100 shadow-sm mb-6">
      <p class="text-med-gold text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.25em] mb-3">July</p>
      <h3 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-4">Blazing Sun and Dry Heat</h3>
      <p class="text-slate-600 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-5">
        The perfect month for sun worshippers. Days offer up to 15 hours of daylight and humidity stays contained.
      </p>
      <ul class="space-y-4">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
          <span class="w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-med-gold flex-shrink-0 mt-2"></span>
          <span class="text-slate-600 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed"><strong>The temperatures.</strong> Daytime averages hover around 88°F (31°C), but if an African anticyclone hits, it easily spikes to 93°F (34°C).</span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
          <span class="w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-med-gold flex-shrink-0 mt-2"></span>
          <span class="text-slate-600 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed"><strong>The evenings.</strong> They drop to a comfortable 72°F (22°C). The sea breeze cools things down just enough for perfect al fresco dining.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
          <span class="w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-med-gold flex-shrink-0 mt-2"></span>
          <span class="text-slate-600 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed"><strong>The sea.</strong> Gorgeous water at 77°F (25°C). Ideal for floating for hours without feeling cold when you get out.</span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="bg-white p-8 rounded-[2rem] border border-slate-100 shadow-sm">
      <p class="text-med-gold text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.25em] mb-3">August</p>
      <h3 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-4">Peak Heat (and Humidity)</h3>
      <p class="text-slate-600 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-5">
        In August, the heat trapped by the limestone cliffs really kicks in, and humidity climbs to 65-70%.
      </p>
      <ul class="space-y-4">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
          <span class="w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-med-gold flex-shrink-0 mt-2"></span>
          <span class="text-slate-600 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed"><strong>The temperatures.</strong> The thermometer stabilizes at 90-91°F (32-33°C), but the mugginess makes it feel much hotter, especially in the narrow alleys.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
          <span class="w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-med-gold flex-shrink-0 mt-2"></span>
          <span class="text-slate-600 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed"><strong>Summer thunderstorms.</strong> Late in August, hot air collides with the warm sea. The result? Short but intense late-afternoon heat storms. They last 20 minutes, cool the air down, and leave you with insane skies afterward.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
          <span class="w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-med-gold flex-shrink-0 mt-2"></span>
          <span class="text-slate-600 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed"><strong>The sea.</strong> Reaches its annual peak at 81°F (27°C). It feels like a thermal bath where you lose track of time.</span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- L'inganno dell'ombra -->
  <div>
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6">The Shade Deception: Why the Landscape Changes Your Plans</h2>
    <p class="text-slate-600 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-8">
      This is the classic rookie mistake that ruins beach days. The mountains of the Coast drop sheer into the sea and block the sunlight earlier than you think. Every stretch of coast has its own shade schedule.
    </p>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
      <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-[2rem] border border-slate-100 shadow-sm">
        <p class="text-med-gold text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.2em] mb-2">Amalfi · Atrani · Minori</p>
        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2 text-lg">Morning sun, shade after 3:30 PM</h4>
        <p class="text-slate-600 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">These beaches face southeast. You get amazing sun in the morning, but around 3:30-4:00 PM the sun disappears behind the mountain and the beach goes into shade. Great if you hate scorching heat, terrible if you want to have an aperitivo in the sun.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-[2rem] border border-slate-100 shadow-sm">
        <p class="text-med-gold text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.2em] mb-2">Positano · Praiano</p>
        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2 text-lg">Sun until evening, Gavitella paradise</h4>
        <p class="text-slate-600 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">Here you are on the opposite side. The sun kisses this coast until late. Praiano, specifically Gavitella beach, gets the longest sunlight on the Coast: you can stay in your swimsuit until 8:00 PM and watch the sun dip straight into the sea behind Capri.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-[2rem] border border-slate-100 shadow-sm">
        <p class="text-med-gold text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.2em] mb-2">Ravello</p>
        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2 text-lg">If you can't breathe, climb</h4>
        <p class="text-slate-600 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">Sitting at 1,200 feet (365 metres), <a href="/en-us/amalfi-coast/ravello/" class="text-med-blue hover:underline">Ravello</a> is always 5-7°F cooler than the beach, with a dry breeze that will save your lungs. The regenerating escape from muggy days.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Valigia & Survival Kit -->
  <div>
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6">Packing and Survival Kit for the Coast</h2>
    <p class="text-slate-600 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Walking the Coast in August is nothing like a casual stroll in Miami. It is a vertical workout, and every wardrobe choice counts.
    </p>

    <div class="bg-white p-8 rounded-[2rem] border border-slate-100 shadow-sm">
      <div class="space-y-6">
        <div>
          <p class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2 text-lg">The shoe drama</p>
          <p class="text-slate-600 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">Leave your heels at the hotel and forget cheap plastic flip-flops for walking. The limestone steps and volcanic stone pavements (especially in Positano) get slick from the mix of heat and sea salt. Wear sturdy flat sandals with rubber soles or canvas sneakers with good grip.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="border-t border-slate-100 pt-6">
          <p class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2 text-lg">Linen, linen, linen</p>
          <p class="text-slate-600 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">Synthetic fabrics will make you sweat double. Opt for loose shirts, dresses and pants made of linen or lightweight cotton. Silk is excellent for evenings. Avoid tight jeans and clingy polos: the Coast lives on relaxed elegance, not restrictive clothing.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="border-t border-slate-100 pt-6">
          <p class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2 text-lg">The killer reflection</p>
          <p class="text-slate-600 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">The white rocks of the Coast reflect light like a mirror. The UV index hits a dangerous 9 or 10. Polarized sunglasses, a wide-brim hat and SPF 50 sunscreen are non-negotiable unless you want to look like a lobster on day one.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 3 Meteo Hack -->
  <div>
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6">The Expert's 3 Weather Hacks to Avoid Losing Your Mind</h2>
    <p class="text-slate-600 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Three local tricks that make the difference between a vacation you survived and a vacation you actually lived.
    </p>

    <div class="space-y-4">
      <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-[2rem] border border-slate-100 shadow-sm">
        <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
          <div class="w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-med-gold/15 flex-shrink-0 flex items-center justify-center text-med-gold font-serif font-bold">1</div>
          <div>
            <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2 text-lg">Ban the buses during peak hours</h4>
            <p class="text-slate-600 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">Taking the <a href="/en-us/guide/sita-bus/" class="text-med-blue hover:underline">SITA bus</a> between 11:00 AM and 3:00 PM in August is an extreme sport. You will wait under a scorching sun and sit through brutal traffic. Use the <a href="/en-us/guide/ferries/" class="text-med-blue hover:underline">Travelmar ferries</a>. They cost more, but the sea breeze turns transit into a refreshing scenic cruise.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-[2rem] border border-slate-100 shadow-sm">
        <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
          <div class="w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-med-gold/15 flex-shrink-0 flex items-center justify-center text-med-gold font-serif font-bold">2</div>
          <div>
            <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2 text-lg">The evening "Faugn"</h4>
            <p class="text-slate-600 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">This is the local name for the hot breeze that blows down from the mountains at night. Sometimes at 11:00 PM, you will get a sudden gust of dry, warm air. Keep this in mind if you take a moonlight boat tour: always pack a light long-sleeve shirt just in case.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-[2rem] border border-slate-100 shadow-sm">
        <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
          <div class="w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-med-gold/15 flex-shrink-0 flex items-center justify-center text-med-gold font-serif font-bold">3</div>
          <div>
            <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2 text-lg">Tactical granita</h4>
            <p class="text-slate-600 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">When the heat gets to you, look for the roadside stands selling fresh slushes made on the spot with real Amalfi Sfusato IGP lemons. It is not just an Instagram cliché: it is a genuine local remedy to reboot your blood pressure and fight stair-induced dehydration.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- I Numeri dell'Estate -->
  <div>
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6">Summer by the Numbers</h2>
    <p class="text-slate-600 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">
      For data lovers, here is the clean summary of July and August 2026 on the Amalfi Coast.
    </p>

    <div class="overflow-hidden rounded-[2rem] border border-slate-100 shadow-sm">
      <table class="w-full text-xs sm:text-sm">
        <thead class="bg-slate-900 text-white">
          <tr>
            <th class="text-left p-5 font-serif font-bold text-med-gold">Weather indicator</th>
            <th class="text-left p-5 font-serif font-bold text-med-gold">July</th>
            <th class="text-left p-5 font-serif font-bold text-med-gold">August</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="bg-white">
          <tr class="border-b border-slate-100">
            <td class="p-5 font-bold">Average daytime high</td>
            <td class="p-5 text-slate-600">88°F (31°C)</td>
            <td class="p-5 text-slate-600">90°F (32°C)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-slate-100">
            <td class="p-5 font-bold">Average nighttime low</td>
            <td class="p-5 text-slate-600">72°F (22°C)</td>
            <td class="p-5 text-slate-600">73°F (23°C)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-slate-100">
            <td class="p-5 font-bold">Sea temperature</td>
            <td class="p-5 text-slate-600">77°F (25°C) fresh</td>
            <td class="p-5 text-slate-600">81°F (27°C) warm</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-slate-100">
            <td class="p-5 font-bold">Days with rain</td>
            <td class="p-5 text-slate-600">Almost none (1 day)</td>
            <td class="p-5 text-slate-600">Rare storms (3 days)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-slate-100">
            <td class="p-5 font-bold">Average humidity</td>
            <td class="p-5 text-slate-600">60% (dry)</td>
            <td class="p-5 text-slate-600">66% (muggy)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-5 font-bold">Actual sunshine hours</td>
            <td class="p-5 text-slate-600">11 hours</td>
            <td class="p-5 text-slate-600">10 hours</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <blockquote class="border-l-4 border-med-gold pl-6 my-10">
      <p class="text-slate-700 text-xl font-serif italic leading-relaxed">
        "The Coast in summer isn't fought, it's followed. Those who know the rhythm of shade and the secret of granita don't suffer the heat: they savour it."
      </p>
    </blockquote>
  </div>

  <!-- Consigli finali -->
  <div class="bg-white p-8 rounded-[2rem] shadow-xl border border-slate-100">
    <h3 class="text-xl font-bold mb-6">Final Tips for Your Summer</h3>
    <div class="space-y-6">
      <div>
        <p class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-1">Plan beaches around the sun schedule</p>
        <p class="text-slate-600 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">If you love full-day sun, alternate: morning in <a href="/en-us/amalfi-coast/amalfi/" class="text-med-blue hover:underline">Amalfi</a> or Atrani, afternoon in <a href="/en-us/amalfi-coast/praiano/" class="text-med-blue hover:underline">Praiano</a> (Gavitella) or Positano. It is the best way to catch every ray.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-1">Drink more than you think you need</p>
        <p class="text-slate-600 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">The stairs of the Coast are a hidden workout. Carry a bottle of at least 750 ml and refill it at the public fountains of each village (the spring water is drinkable and excellent).</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-1">Book your tables in advance</p>
        <p class="text-slate-600 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">From mid-July onward, restaurants with sunset views are packed. Book dinner at least 3 or 4 days ahead, especially if you want the sea-view table in Positano or the seafront table in Amalfi.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

</BlogPostLayout>
